CAT = "s65StateLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("A corporation sells its own newly issued bonds directly to investors without an underwriter. Under the Uniform Securities Act, the corporation is:",
 "An issuer only", ["A broker-dealer", "An investment adviser", "An agent of the buyers"],
 "Issuers are excluded from the broker-dealer definition when they sell their own securities. Individuals who sell for the issuer may still be agents."),
("A broker-dealer has no office in a state and trades there only with insurance companies and banks. In that state it is:",
 "Not a broker-dealer", ["Required to register", "Required to notice file", "An agent of those firms"],
 "A broker-dealer with no place of business in a state whose only clients there are institutions, issuers or other broker-dealers falls outside the state's definition."),
("Under the Uniform Securities Act, which can be registered as an agent?",
 "Only an individual", ["A corporation", "A limited partnership", "A broker-dealer"],
 "An agent is an individual who represents a broker-dealer or an issuer in buying or selling securities. Firms register as broker-dealers or advisers, not agents."),
("A company's payroll manager helps fellow employees buy company stock through a plan and receives no commission. She is:",
 "Not an agent", ["An agent of the issuer", "A broker-dealer", "An investment adviser"],
 "An individual representing an issuer in sales to its own employees, with no commission or other pay for soliciting, is excluded from the agent definition."),
("Which is NOT a security under the Uniform Securities Act?",
 "A fixed annuity", ["A variable annuity", "A limited partnership unit", "A stock warrant"],
 "Fixed annuities and traditional insurance policies are insurance products, with the insurer bearing the investment risk. Variable annuities and LP units are securities."),
("Investors give money to a promoter who will raise and sell cattle and share the profits with them. This arrangement is most likely:",
 "An investment contract", ["A commodity, not a security", "A partnership loan", "A futures contract"],
 "Under the Howey test, an investment of money in a common enterprise with profits expected from others' efforts is an investment contract, and so a security."),
("An issuer is registering a national offering with the SEC and wants it effective in a state at the same time. It should use registration by:",
 "Coordination", ["Qualification", "Notification", "Private placement"],
 "Registration by coordination is for issues also registering with the SEC under the Securities Act of 1933, and becomes effective in the state when the federal registration does."),
("A local company sells shares only within its own state and does not register with the SEC. It registers in the state by:",
 "Qualification", ["Coordination", "Notice filing", "Exemption"],
 "Qualification is the method for offerings not registered with the SEC, and it gives the Administrator the most control over the filing."),
("Which security is exempt from state registration?",
 "A Canadian province's bonds", ["A small company's common stock", "A limited partnership interest", "A non-traded REIT"],
 "Securities of the U.S. government, U.S. municipalities, and Canadian and other foreign governments the U.S. recognizes are exempt securities."),
("Under the Uniform Securities Act, the private placement exemption limits offers in the state, in any 12 months, to no more than:",
 "10 non-institutional persons", ["35 non-institutional persons", "25 persons of any kind", "5 non-institutional persons"],
 "The state private placement exemption allows offers to at most 10 non-institutional persons in 12 months, with no commission for the solicitation and a buyer's investment intent."),
("A customer calls an agent and asks to buy a stock that is not registered in the state. To claim the unsolicited order exemption, the Administrator may require:",
 "A signed client statement", ["Registration of the stock", "A legal opinion", "Approval by FINRA"],
 "An unsolicited nonissuer transaction is exempt, and the Administrator may require the client to confirm in writing that the order was not solicited."),
("A state Administrator learns an adviser is about to mail misleading offers to clients. To stop it at once, the Administrator may:",
 "Issue a cease and desist", ["Order the adviser jailed", "Fine it without any hearing", "Seize its client accounts"],
 "The Administrator may issue a cease and desist order, even before a hearing, when a violation is occurring or about to occur. Jail requires a criminal court."),
("A state Administrator wants a permanent injunction against a firm. It must:",
 "Ask a court for one", ["Issue it as an order", "Get SEC approval", "Ask FINRA to act"],
 "The Administrator issues orders such as cease and desist, but injunctions and restraining orders come from a court, which the Administrator can petition."),
("A client bought an unregistered nonexempt security on an IAR's recommendation and received dividends. If she sues under the Act, she can generally recover:",
 "Price, interest, fees, less income", ["Triple damages plus her legal fees", "The price paid minus any legal costs", "Only the dividends she gave up"],
 "Civil liability lets the buyer recover the consideration paid, plus interest and reasonable attorney's fees, less any income received, on tender of the security."),
("A firm sold a security in violation of the Act and makes a written rescission offer. If the client does not accept within 30 days, she:",
 "Loses her right to sue", ["Gets a second offer", "Recovers triple damages", "Keeps all her rights"],
 "A proper rescission offer, rejected or not accepted within 30 days, ends the buyer's right to sue over that sale."),
("A person who violates the Uniform Securities Act can face criminal penalties if the violation was:",
 "Willful", ["Unintentional", "Reported by the firm", "Under a dollar limit"],
 "Criminal penalties apply to willful violations, through prosecution in court. The Administrator may refer evidence to the prosecutor but cannot jail anyone."),
]
